{"question": "An investigator conducts a study to determine whether earlier detection of glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) in patients increases survival time. One subset of study participants consists of asymptomatic individuals who were diagnosed with GBM after undergoing a screening MRI of the brain. The other subset of study participants was diagnosed with GBM only after they developed symptoms. Results from the study show that the asymptomatic patients who were diagnosed with screening MRI had an average survival time that was 6 weeks longer than that of the patients who were diagnosed after symptom onset. Which of the following statistical biases is most likely to have occurred as a result of the endpoint selected for this study?", "options": {"A": "Observer-expectancy bias", "B": "Length-time bias", "C": "Confounding bias", "D": "Surveillance bias", "E": "Lead-time bias"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Oncology", "Neurology", "Epidemiology", "Biostatistics", "Clinical Research"], "option_domains": ["Biostatistics and Epidemiology", "Clinical Research Bias"], "question_analyses": {"Oncology": "The medical scenario described involves a study comparing the survival times of two groups of patients with glioblastoma multiforme (GBM): one group diagnosed through asymptomatic screening MRI and another diagnosed after symptom onset. The study found that asymptomatic patients had an average survival time 6 weeks longer than those diagnosed after symptom onset. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\nGlioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is a highly aggressive form of brain cancer with a poor prognosis. It is characterized by rapid growth and a tendency to spread within the brain. Early detection through screening can potentially lead to earlier intervention and better outcomes. However, the study's findings need to be critically evaluated for potential biases. ### Noteworthy Aspects and Potential Biases:\n1. **Lead Time Bias**: This is the most likely statistical bias in this scenario. Lead time bias occurs when the early detection of a disease through screening does not change the actual course of the disease but merely extends the period from diagnosis to death. In this case, asymptomatic patients diagnosed through screening MRI might have a longer survival time simply because their diagnosis is made earlier, even though the disease progression and ultimate outcome remain the same. This can create a false impression that early detection improves survival. 2. **Length Bias**: This bias can also be present, where screening tends to detect slower-growing tumors more frequently than faster-growing ones..", "Neurology": "The medical scenario described involves a study comparing the survival times of two groups of patients with glioblastoma multiforme (GBM): one group diagnosed through asymptomatic screening MRI and another diagnosed after symptom onset. The study found that patients diagnosed through screening MRI had an average survival time 6 weeks longer than those diagnosed after symptom onset. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\nGlioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is a highly aggressive form of brain cancer that typically presents with symptoms such as headaches, seizures, and cognitive changes. The condition is characterized by rapid progression and poor prognosis, with median survival times ranging from 12 to 18 months with standard treatment. ### Noteworthy Aspects and Potential Statistical Bias:\n1. **Lead Time Bias**: This is the most likely statistical bias in this scenario. Lead time bias occurs when the early detection of a disease through screening leads to an apparent increase in survival time, not because the disease is being treated more effectively, but because the time of diagnosis is moved forward. In this case, patients diagnosed through screening MRI are detected earlier, but the actual progression of the disease and its ultimate outcome (death) remain unchanged. The 6-week increase in survival t