 known as Brown-S\u00e9quard syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by a hemisection (half-section) of the spinal cord, typically resulting from trauma or, in this case, possibly from a complication of the neck manipulation. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n- **Symptoms**: The patient's left-sided weakness is indicative of a motor deficit on the ipsilateral (same side) side of the lesion. This is a hallmark of Brown-S\u00e9quard syndrome. - **MRI Findings**: The T2-weighted MRI showing a left C5 hemicord lesion supports the diagnosis of a spinal cord injury at that level. - **Management**: The patient is treated with cervical immobilization, a course of steroids, and physical therapy, which are standard interventions for spinal cord injuries to prevent further damage and promote recovery. ### Noteworthy Aspects:\n1. **Acute Onset After Manipulation**: The sudden onset of symptoms following a chiropractic neck manipulation is alarming. It suggests a possible traumatic injury to the spinal cord, which could be due to excessive force or improper technique during the manipulation. 2..", "Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation": "The scenario describes a 40-year-old patient who developed left-sided weakness following a chiropractic neck manipulation. The patient's presentation, along with the MRI findings of a T2-weighted lesion at the left C5 hemicord, suggests a condition known as Brown-S\u00e9quard syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by a hemisection (half-section) of the spinal cord, typically resulting from trauma or, in this case, possibly from a complication of the chiropractic manipulation. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n- **Symptoms**: The patient's left-sided weakness is indicative of a motor deficit on the ipsilateral (same side) side of the lesion. This is a hallmark of Brown-S\u00e9quard syndrome. - **MRI Findings**: The T2-weighted MRI showing a left C5 hemicord lesion supports the diagnosis of a spinal cord injury at that level. - **Manage